                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

Overall, your house it pretty air tight, however there are some nuisance areas where air leakage occurs. Air leakage was observed around the base of the frame from a few of the upstairs windows. I recommend using caulking from the interior to seal any gaps between the inserts and sills. 



Another area air leakage was observed is along the crawlspace walls below the main entrance. This may explain why you notice that this floor is typically cold. The poly along these walls appear to not be sealed very well along the floor header. I recommend you seal the poly along the header by using acoustical caulking (and tape), while ensuring the poly is sealed without a lot of excess (as you will notice on the interior corner, there is a lot of excess poly that has been bunched up, which makes it difficult to seal). 



Air leakage was also observed through all three bathroom exhaust fans, as I suspect the dampers in the units are not sealing. I recommend you replace your exhaust fans with an ENERGY STAR® rated model.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 90&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;3.0 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a home energy evaluation, which includes an air leakage test, before and after your retrofits. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 studs with 6 inches (R20) of fiberglass batt insulation, as well as 2x2 strapping with no insulation. I have modelled adding 3 inches (R15) of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding R15 of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 530&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;17.7 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,875 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;













&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</mainWalls>
                <foundation>

The floor of your crawl space appears to be concrete, as well as roughly half exposed bedrock. I have modeled adding R6.5 of spray foam to the crawlspace floor (equivalent to adding 2" of spray foam only to the bedrock, or R13). 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding 2 inches of spray foam to the bedrock in the crawlspace, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 70&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;2.3 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 

































&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</foundation>
                <windows>

All of your windows are triple pane ones that do not appear to have a Low E coating or an insulating gas fill between the panes of glass, I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading all of them with ENERGY STAR® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing all11 windows with triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STAR® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 250&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;8.5 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 





























&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</windows>
                <VentilationSystem>

During my assessment, there appeared to be minimal ventilation for your home. Proper ventilation protects a home from unwanted toxins, pollutants, and odors and can also help protect the overall structure of a home or building by eliminating excess moisture from the air. An energy-efficient heat recovery ventilator is one of the best ways to control indoor air quality.



An HRV saves on energy costs compared to conventional ventilation systems because it recovers heat from exhausted air.  The HRV draws in stale, indoor air and passes it through a heat exchanger. The exchanger captures most of the heat before it exhausts the stale air outside. 



At the same time, the HRV draws outdoor air in for filtering, passes it through the other half of the heat exchanger where it collects heat from the exhausted air, and finally distributes the outdoor air throughout the house. The warmed, outdoor air is distributed through an existing forced-air distribution system or a dedicated ductwork system. 



Ideally, an HRV will pull air from the rooms that produce moisture (kitchen &amp; bathrooms), while supplying fresh air to the remainder of the home. An HRV could be designed into the existing duct work from the fan coil that supplies heat to the upstairs. However, some ducts may need to be installed to incorporate fresh air to the main floor as well. 



Replacing the bathroom fans with ENERGY STAR® rated models, and controlling them with a humidistat will help remove any localized humidity. Currently, your humidistat controls the bathroom fan in the ensuite off the master bedroom. As this is the furthest room from the kitchen/living room (as well as down stairs), this fan may not be effectively removing moisture from the home. Each bathroom fan could be connected to it's own humidistat, therefore controlling moisture locally, instead of running all 3 simultaneously. 



I recommend that you have an ENERGY STAR® rated Heat Recovery Ventilator (HRV) system designed and installed by an individual who holds current certification from an organization, such as HRAI.



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</VentilationSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your current oil fired boiler was originally rated at 85.3% AFUE. When it is time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with a minimum efficiency of 92% AFUE. If you choose a high-efficiency condensing propane boiler, the current chimney can be eliminated altogether.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with a high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with an efficiency of 92% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 600&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;16.5 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;







&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Domestic Hot Water&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



Currently, your Oil-fired boiler is used to heat your hot water during the winter months and electricity is used to heat your hot water during the summer months. (Winter is assumed to be 9 months of the year).



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By using your Oil-fired boiler year-round to heat your hot water, you could potentially save $ 250 per year in energy savings.



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Wood Fireplace Savings&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



&lt;b&gt;At $ 341 per cord&lt;/b&gt;

By using your wood fireplace for 50% of your heating load, you could potentially save $ 400 per year in energy savings.



&lt;b&gt;At $ 100 per cord&lt;/b&gt; (estimated cost of harvesting your own firewood)

By using your wood fireplace for 50% of your heating load, you could potentially save $ 1,100 per year in energy savings.



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Yellowknife&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $19.45 min, $0.32 per kWh

Oil  - $1.12 per litre 

Propane - $0.71 per litre

Wood - $341 per cord 

Pellets - $5.67 per bag



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Disclaimer&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Your Energy Advisor&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



&lt;b&gt;Michael Goodwin

Energy Management Specialist&lt;/b&gt;

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: michael.goodwin@aea.nt.ca



&lt;font color="lightseagreen"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportation costs, and climate change!&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/font&gt;



&lt;font color="yellowgreen"&gt;&lt;hr/&gt;&lt;/font&gt;

</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
        </Options>
